{"name":"Ginger Beer","hex":"#c27f38","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ginger beer","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fermented, typically non-alcoholic, drink flavoured with ginger.","A glass or drink of ginger beer."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["queer (i.e. homosexual)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ginger_beer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ginger beer","blocks":[{"text":"Ginger beer is a sweetened and carbonated, usually non-alcoholic, type of soft drink. Historically it was a type of beer brewed by the natural fermentation of prepared ginger spice, yeast and sugar. Modern ginger beers are often manufactured rather than brewed, frequently with flavour and colour additives, with artificial carbonation. The related ginger ales also are not brewed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Ginger_beer_bottle_assortment.jpg/330px-Ginger_beer_bottle_assortment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginger_beer","wikidata":"Q1448819"},{"title":"Ginger","blocks":[{"text":"Ginger is a flowering plant whose rhizome, ginger root or ginger, is widely used as a spice and a folk medicine. It is an herbaceous perennial that grows annual pseudostems about one meter tall, bearing narrow leaf blades. The inflorescences bear flowers having pale yellow petals with purple edges, and arise directly from the rhizome on separate shoots."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Koeh-146-no_text.jpg/330px-Koeh-146-no_text.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginger","wikidata":"Q35625"},{"title":"Beer","blocks":[{"text":"Beer is an alcoholic beverage produced by the brewing and fermentation of starches from cereal grain—most commonly malted barley, although wheat, maize, rice, and oats are also used. The grain is mashed to convert starch in the grain to sugars, which dissolve in water to form wort. Fermentation of the wort by yeast produces ethanol and carbonation in the beer. Beer is one of the oldest and most widely consumed alcoholic drinks in the world, and one of the most popular of all drinks. Most modern beer is brewed with hops, which add bitterness and other flavours and act as a natural preservative and stabilizer. Other flavouring agents, such as gruit, herbs, or fruits, may be included or used instead of hops. In commercial brewing, natural carbonation is often replaced with forced carbonation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Jeju_beer_09_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Jeju_beer_09_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beer","wikidata":"Q44"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Refreshments_stall_before_1914.jpg/500px-Refreshments_stall_before_1914.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Refreshments_stall_before_1914.jpg/500px-Refreshments_stall_before_1914.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"R. White's soft drinks, including ginger beer, sold in England in the early 1900s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARefreshments_stall_before_1914.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Bottle%2C_ginger_beer_%2851360080511%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Bottle%2C_ginger_beer_%2851360080511%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Bottle%2C_ginger_beer_%2851360080511%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Bottle%2C_ginger_beer_%2851360080511%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://th